  "rule": "A vector is valid when its signature verifies over the bytes of \"message\" for \"namespace\" by the rules of BRS 73.2 and the key inside the signature has the stated \"key_type\" and \"fingerprint\" (BRS 73.3, items 1 and 2). For an invalid vector \"reason\" names the failure the reference implementation reports (verifyRecordSignature in src/sign/recordSignature.ts; \"key_rejected\" is followed by the reason the key was refused for); \"key_mismatch\" means the signature itself is sound and its key is not the stated one. \"hash_algorithm\" and \"signature_algorithm\" repeat the two algorithm names the signature carries.",
  "notes": [
    "Every message is a record digest as BRS 73.2 defines the signed message: 71 ASCII bytes, no line terminator.",
    "Vectors with \"signed_by\": \"ssh-keygen\" were made by the OpenSSH named in \"generated_with\" from the fixture keys under test/fixtures/keys/, which are public test data and protect nothing.",
    "Vectors with \"signed_by\": \"fixture-signer\" were made in process by test/support/sshSigner.ts. They cover what ssh-keygen cannot produce on a machine without a hardware token, or at all: the two sk- key types (simulated as OpenSSH PROTOCOL.u2f describes a token signature), an RSA signature whose algorithm does not follow the message hash, and the refused ssh-rsa (SHA-1). The contract test proves that ssh-keygen -Y verify accepts each valid one of them.",
    "With an RSA key the ssh-keygen named in \"generated_with\" signs with rsa-sha2-512 over a sha512 message hash and with rsa-sha2-256 over a sha256 message hash (-O hashalg=sha256). Verification treats the message hash and the RSA signature algorithm as independent choices.",
    "ECDSA signatures are randomised: regenerating this file changes them."
  ],
